---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Set Up 2FA on PayPal (TOTP Authenticator)';

const description =
  'Enable TOTP two-factor authentication on PayPal without a mobile app. Get the setup key from PayPal security settings and generate live, browser-based codes.';

---

<GuideLayout
  serviceName="PayPal"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/guides/paypal/"
  dateModified="2026-11-10"
  lastReviewed="2026-11-10"
  intro="PayPal supports standard TOTP as its authenticator-app option for 2-step verification — stronger than the default SMS codes, because a phone number can be hijacked by a SIM swap and money flows through PayPal. Worth doing: check which methods stay enrolled after setup, so no weaker fallback lingers without your say-so."
  minutes={4}
  params={['6 digits', '30s period', 'SHA-1']}
  ctaTitle="Your PayPal account is a payment rail — treat the backup like one"
  ctaBody="PayPal's recovery flow can lean on the phone number on file, which is exactly the factor SIM-swap attackers target. Save PayPal's recovery codes in an encrypted password manager and review enrolled 2-step-verification methods once a quarter."
  steps={[
    {
      title: 'Open PayPal security settings',
      body: 'Sign in at paypal.com → click the gear icon (Settings) → “Security”. Find the “2-step verification” section. Business accounts may find it under Account Settings instead of Profile Settings.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Start 2-step verification setup',
      body: 'Click “Set Up” (or “Update”) next to 2-step verification. PayPal offers SMS and “Use an authenticator app” — choose the authenticator app option. It is the standard TOTP factor, independent of your phone number.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Reveal the secret',
      body: 'PayPal shows a QR code plus a Base32 “secret key” line for manual entry. Copy the key — or screenshot the QR and paste the image into the page, the parser reads it locally.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Generate, verify, activate',
      body: 'Paste into 2facounter.com, copy the live code, and paste it back into PayPal’s confirmation field. 2-step verification flips to active and PayPal starts asking for a code at sign-in.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Audit your methods',
      body: 'Back on the 2-step verification screen, check every enrolled method. If SMS is still listed as a second method, decide deliberately whether to keep it — it’s a weaker fallback than a saved recovery code. If PayPal offers recovery codes, download them now.',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Does PayPal require 2FA?',
      a: 'Personal accounts can enable it voluntarily, and PayPal encourages 2-step verification on accounts that move money. Whatever your account type, enabling it on a money-mover account is one of the highest-value few minutes in personal security.',
    },
    {
      q: 'SMS codes vs authenticator app on PayPal — which should I pick?',
      a: 'Authenticator app, always. SMS codes die with your SIM: a SIM-swap attacker who ports your phone number receives your codes. TOTP lives on the device (or in this browser) and never transmits anything.',
    },
    {
      q: 'I lost my phone and can’t get PayPal codes — what now?',
      a: 'If you saved recovery codes, use one at sign-in. If SMS was kept as a fallback, that still works. If neither exists, PayPal support verifies your identity through account details and restores access — which is slower and more painful than saving recovery codes today.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Codes rejected right after setup — what broke?',
      a: 'Most rejections are clock drift: PayPal’s server and your device disagree on what “now” is, and the code rolls over mid-verification. Sync your system clock automatically, then submit the freshly displayed code. If a wrong code ever gets through and you lock yourself out, PayPal’s recovery path exists — but a synced clock prevents that in the first place.',
    },
  ]}
/>
